<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hebrides</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Western Isles</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hebrides are the islands off the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-hebrides">Inner Hebrides</a>, such as Skye, Mull and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islay">Islay</a>, and the Outer Hebrides, such as Lewis and Harris. Settled by Norse from the 9th century, they belonged to the Kingdom of the Isles under Norwegian overlordship until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-perth">Treaty of Perth</a> of 1266 ceded them to Scotland. Gaelic language and culture remain strongest there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hebrides.jpg" alt="Hebrides" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clydiee · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hebrides_topographic_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Hebrides are the largest archipelago in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>, with a combined area of 7,285 square kilometres. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-hebrides">Inner Hebrides</a>, closer to the mainland, include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islay">Islay</a>, Jura, Skye, Mull, Raasay, Staffa and the Small Isles. The Outer Hebrides form a chain of more than 100 islands and skerries about 70 kilometres west of the mainland, among them Lewis and Harris, North Uist, Benbecula, South Uist and Barra. Their rocks range from Precambrian strata, among the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, to Paleogene igneous intrusions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>, around 77 CE, wrote that there were 30 Hebudes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The islands were settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> era around 6500 BCE or earlier. The standing stones at Callanish date to the 3rd millennium BCE, and Cladh Hallan, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> settlement on South Uist, is the only site in the UK where prehistoric mummies have been found. Scottish Gaelic arrived from Ireland with the growing influence of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> from the 6th century CE.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids began towards the end of the 8th century, and in the following decades the Hebrides came under Norse control and settlement, especially after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a>'s victory at Hafrsfjord in 872. The Norse called the islands Suðreyjar, the Southern Isles. In 1098 Edgar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> formally signed the islands over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-barefoot">Magnus III of Norway</a>, who had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, the Hebrides and the Isle of Man in a swift campaign. His skald Bjorn Cripplehand recorded that in the Uists the king dyed his sword red in blood.</p><p>As the Norse era closed, Norse-speaking princes gave way to Gaelic-speaking clan chiefs, including the MacLeods of Lewis and Harris and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clan-donald">Clan Donald</a>. By the early 14th century the MacDonald Lords of the Isles, based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islay">Islay</a>, were in theory their feudal superiors. James IV forfeited their lands in 1493. Many islesmen supported the Jacobite risings of 1715 and 1745, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-culloden">Culloden</a> the clan system was broken up and the islands became a series of landed estates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The culture of the inhabitants has been successively influenced by Celtic-speaking, Norse-speaking and English-speaking peoples, and the names of the islands derive from the different languages spoken there. Much of Scottish Gaelic literature and music has historically originated in the Hebrides. The economy depends on crofting, fishing, tourism, the oil industry and renewable energy. The islands have less biodiversity than mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, but a significant number of seals and seabirds.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hebrides” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 795 Viking raids struck the <b>Hebrides</b>, and Iona was attacked repeatedly; in 806 sixty-eight members of the community were killed, and a new monastery was built at Kells in Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Magnus Barefoot campaigned in the <b>Hebrides</b>, Man and Ireland and died in Ulster in 1103.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Galloway, Moray and the Norse-ruled north and west were brought under royal control only slowly: after the Battle of Largs in 1263, the Treaty of Perth of 1266 transferred the <b>Western Isles</b> and the Isle of Man from Norway to Scotland, and Orkney and Shetland followed in 1468–1469.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Norse settlers took Orkney, Shetland, the <b>Hebrides</b> and the Isle of Man.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In his reign, later remembered as a golden age, Scotland gained the <b>Western Isles</b> and the Isle of Man from Norway by the Treaty of Perth.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-13">13 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Haakon withdrew, and in 1266 his successor ceded the <b>Hebrides</b> and Man to Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-2">2 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
